WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.367 --> 00:00:02.035
♪ Baby, can't you see ♪

00:00:03.803 --> 00:00:05.872
Rewind to 2003.

00:00:05.872 --> 00:00:09.509
Britney Spears hasn't had a
big hit in almost four years,

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:11.811
and critics say her career is slumping.

00:00:11.811 --> 00:00:15.181
This all would change when
she dropped her song "Toxic."

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:16.683
♪ It's dangerous ♪

00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:18.184
♪ I'm falling ♪

00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:23.656
["Tere Mere Beech Mein"]

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.396
The real story behind "Toxic"

00:00:29.396 --> 00:00:31.865
begins in 1981 in India

00:00:31.865 --> 00:00:34.467
with a blockbuster Bollywood musical.

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:36.936
Those high-pitched strings in the score?

00:00:36.936 --> 00:00:38.371
You've heard them before,

00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:39.939
at the opening of Toxic,

00:00:42.642 --> 00:00:44.310
then repeated throughout the song.

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:48.014
It's this musical number,

00:00:48.014 --> 00:00:49.616
"Tere Mere Beech Mein,"

00:00:49.616 --> 00:00:53.253
that's the source of "Toxic"'s
unmistakable hook and riff

00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:55.555
and a lot of the song's intensity.

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:57.590
Manasi Prasad: "Tere Mere
Beech Mein," it's from

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:01.628
a classic film called "Ek Duuje Ke Liye."

00:01:01.628 --> 00:01:03.530
Narrator: This is Manasi Prasad,

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:05.465
a classical Carnatic vocalist

00:01:05.465 --> 00:01:08.701
with an encyclopedic
knowledge of Indian music.

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:10.837
Prasad: This song is actually based

00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:14.407
on an Indian classical rāga, or melody.

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:15.375
It's

00:01:15.375 --> 00:01:16.976
♪ La la la ♪

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:19.846
♪ La la la la la la ♪

00:01:19.846 --> 00:01:21.181
♪ La la ♪

00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:23.883
So you'll find a lot of other Indian songs

00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:26.119
which are in this rāga.

00:01:26.119 --> 00:01:28.021
It's used to convey feelings

00:01:28.021 --> 00:01:31.524
of very intense emotions.

00:01:31.524 --> 00:01:32.759
I think one of the reasons

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:36.196
why, you know, Bollywood
songs work so well

00:01:36.196 --> 00:01:39.466
is the range or the pitch of the songs

00:01:39.466 --> 00:01:41.134
tends to be really high,

00:01:41.134 --> 00:01:43.970
and it brings an intensity
and a brightness.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:45.572
Narrator: Britney's team of songwriters,

00:01:45.572 --> 00:01:47.607
led by hit maker Cathy Dennis,

00:01:47.607 --> 00:01:50.443
took two tiny sections
of the musical number

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:53.146
and expertly merged those
two sections together.

00:01:53.146 --> 00:01:57.417
["Tere Mere Beech Mein"]

00:01:57.417 --> 00:01:59.953
["Tere Mere Beech Mein"]

00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:03.323
["Toxic"]

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:08.161
Adam Ragusea: There's a
string hook that's got

00:02:08.161 --> 00:02:10.063
the low, fast part, which is the

00:02:10.063 --> 00:02:11.631
♪ Bum bum ba da da da dum ba da da dum ♪

00:02:11.631 --> 00:02:13.800
and then there's a very
high keening part that goes

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.302
♪ Da da ba ♪

00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:18.438
Narrator: This is Adam Ragusea,

00:02:18.438 --> 00:02:19.873
a writer and YouTuber

00:02:19.873 --> 00:02:22.675
who's also a classically trained composer.

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:25.311
That second part of the
hook that Adam just sang?

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:29.349
It starts on a superhigh F sharp,

00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:32.919
a note that lies far outside
the song's key of C minor,

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:35.455
making the whole thing sound disorienting.

00:02:35.455 --> 00:02:37.857
Ragusea: That screaming F-sharp note

00:02:37.857 --> 00:02:40.727
makes the whole thing sound cinematic

00:02:40.727 --> 00:02:43.029
and scary in a fun way,

00:02:43.029 --> 00:02:44.430
like an action film.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:45.765
Narrator: There's definitely something

00:02:45.765 --> 00:02:48.601
that sounds dangerous
in that string sample.

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:51.671
This has a lot to do with
the film it was lifted from,

00:02:51.671 --> 00:02:53.740
a tale of forbidden romance.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.375
Prasad: It was a story

00:02:55.375 --> 00:02:58.444
about these star-crossed lovers.

00:02:58.444 --> 00:03:00.346
They fell in love, and it was opposed

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:02.615
by their families,

00:03:02.615 --> 00:03:04.384
so both of them ended up dying

00:03:04.384 --> 00:03:05.318
at the end of the movie.

00:03:05.318 --> 00:03:07.153
So it's kind of a really tragic,

00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:09.622
intense love story.

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:11.724
Narrator: In the movie, love is danger,

00:03:11.724 --> 00:03:13.927
and that's reflected in the music,

00:03:13.927 --> 00:03:15.895
especially in the glissandos,

00:03:15.895 --> 00:03:17.730
these sliding figures in the strings,

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.265
which are pretty much the signature

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:21.201
of "Toxic"'s sampled hook.

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:27.540
They sound slippery and precarious,

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.209
which fits since Britney's singing

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:31.311
about slipping under and falling.

00:03:31.311 --> 00:03:32.946
♪ It's dangerous ♪

00:03:32.946 --> 00:03:35.315
♪ I'm falling ♪

00:03:35.315 --> 00:03:37.951
Narrator: Somehow, these notes
sound even more treacherous

00:03:37.951 --> 00:03:39.619
when the sample's played backwards

00:03:39.619 --> 00:03:41.187
halfway through the track.

00:03:44.958 --> 00:03:46.926
Beyond the screechy orchestral parts

00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:48.361
borrowed from Bollywood,

00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:50.697
the other big source of danger in "Toxic"

00:03:50.697 --> 00:03:53.866
comes from the buzzing,
ominous-sounding guitar.

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:55.835
♪ I'm addicted to you ♪

00:03:55.835 --> 00:04:00.773
♪ Don't you know that you're toxic ♪

00:04:00.773 --> 00:04:02.308
♪ And I love what you do ♪

00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:04.911
♪ Don't you know that you're toxic ♪

00:04:08.047 --> 00:04:09.249
Narrator: If that guitar bit sounds

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:10.717
kind of old-school to you,

00:04:10.717 --> 00:04:12.318
that's because it's surf,

00:04:12.318 --> 00:04:14.187
a rock subgenre that was biggest

00:04:14.187 --> 00:04:16.489
in the late '50s and early '60s,

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:18.057
when there was a boom of interest

00:04:18.057 --> 00:04:19.892
in West Coast wave culture.

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:21.094
Clip: Surfing!

00:04:21.094 --> 00:04:23.196
[surf guitar]

00:04:24.797 --> 00:04:26.165
Narrator: Surf guitar is played in a way

00:04:26.165 --> 00:04:29.235
that conjures up the
sounds and feel of surfing

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:31.404
with a hard and fast picking style

00:04:31.404 --> 00:04:33.506
and loud, wet-sounding reverb.

00:04:33.506 --> 00:04:35.575
♪ Never been ♪

00:04:39.545 --> 00:04:40.913
Narrator: If surf's supposed to capture

00:04:40.913 --> 00:04:43.283
the carefree California ethos,

00:04:43.283 --> 00:04:45.551
you might be wondering
why the guitar in "Toxic"

00:04:45.551 --> 00:04:47.820
has very much the opposite feel.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:51.190
That deep, low guitar
tone sounds suspenseful

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:53.159
in a way that matches the adrenaline rush

00:04:53.159 --> 00:04:55.094
of the Bollywood strings.

00:04:55.094 --> 00:04:58.398
That's probably because you
identify that twangy guitar

00:04:58.398 --> 00:05:00.466
with something else entirely,

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:03.703
one of the most recognizable
movie themes in the world.

00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:06.539
["James Bond Theme"]

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:12.745
The first-ever Bond film, "Dr. No,"

00:05:12.745 --> 00:05:14.881
came out in 1962,

00:05:14.881 --> 00:05:16.649
right in surf rock's heyday.

00:05:16.649 --> 00:05:19.018
So it's not surprising
that its title song,

00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:21.988
which would set the template
for all later Bond themes,

00:05:21.988 --> 00:05:23.589
was fueled by surf.

00:05:25.692 --> 00:05:28.594
The 007 theme had an outsized influence

00:05:28.594 --> 00:05:31.264
on the music of all the Cold War spy films

00:05:31.264 --> 00:05:33.132
and TV series that followed it.

00:05:33.132 --> 00:05:35.935
["Secret Agent Man"]

00:05:42.108 --> 00:05:46.279
♪ There's a man who
leads a life of danger ♪

00:05:46.279 --> 00:05:49.048
By now, the tone of guitar
that you hear in "Toxic"

00:05:49.048 --> 00:05:51.050
has big spy connotations,

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:53.086
and the song's Bond-inspired music video

00:05:53.086 --> 00:05:56.155
seems to be a nod to this connection.

00:05:56.155 --> 00:05:58.191
OK, so we've got the aggressive twang

00:05:58.191 --> 00:05:59.459
of the surf guitar

00:05:59.459 --> 00:06:02.428
evoking the reckless
daring of a special agent

00:06:02.428 --> 00:06:04.664
and frantically sliding Bollywood strings

00:06:04.664 --> 00:06:07.367
capturing the rush of a risky love affair.

00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:09.502
How is it that these two disparate sounds

00:06:09.502 --> 00:06:11.471
from opposite sides of the globe

00:06:11.471 --> 00:06:14.307
seem to have such an
affinity for each other?

00:06:14.307 --> 00:06:17.510
Turns out, they share some
of the same musical DNA.

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:21.614
Monty Norman, the film composer
who penned the 007 theme,

00:06:21.614 --> 00:06:24.250
lifted its melody from a
previous piece he'd written

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:27.487
for a play adaptation of
"A House for Mr. Biswas,"

00:06:27.487 --> 00:06:30.690
VS Naipaul's novel
about Indian immigrants.

00:06:47.273 --> 00:06:50.543
Basically, the sound that
has come to define spy music

00:06:50.543 --> 00:06:53.446
was in fact born of Indian music.

00:06:53.446 --> 00:06:56.115
Ragusea: And so, when we
hear those two aesthetics

00:06:56.115 --> 00:06:57.950
put together in "Toxic,"

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:00.386
it feels really natural to us.

00:07:00.386 --> 00:07:02.155
Narrator: "Toxic"
distills some of the most

00:07:02.155 --> 00:07:04.290
thrilling sounds of global cinema

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:07.293
into a three-minute pop masterpiece.

00:07:07.293 --> 00:07:09.295
What other club banger combines Bond

00:07:09.295 --> 00:07:11.097
and Bollywood so perfectly

00:07:11.097 --> 00:07:14.467
and remains a DJ staple 15 years on?

00:07:14.467 --> 00:07:17.270
That is why "Toxic" is not
only the greatest triumph

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:18.671
of Britney's career,

00:07:18.671 --> 00:07:22.308
but one of the most impressive
pop hits of our time.

00:07:24.177 --> 00:07:25.812
♪ With the taste of your lips ♪

00:07:25.812 --> 00:07:28.181
♪ I'm on a ride ♪

00:07:28.181 --> 00:07:29.582
♪ You're toxic ♪

00:07:29.582 --> 00:07:30.917
♪ I'm slipping under ♪

00:07:30.917 --> 00:07:33.920
♪ With the taste of a poison paradise ♪

